# The Weaver of Dreams

In the time before time, when the world was young and sleep was unknown, there lived a celestial weaver named Morphea. She dwelt in a palace of twilight, suspended between the realm of waking and the void of nothing.

One day, the first humans cried out in exhaustion, for they knew no rest. Their eyes burned with endless sight, their minds raced without pause. Morphea heard their suffering and felt compassion stir in her ancient heart.

She gathered threads of starlight and shadow, weaving them upon her cosmic loom. With each pass of her shuttle, she created the first dreams—tapestries of wonder and mystery. She wove landscapes of impossible beauty, memories that never were, and adventures in lands that existed only in her imagination.

When her work was complete, Morphea descended and touched each human's brow. "Close your eyes," she whispered, "and I shall give you my gift."

And so sleep came to humanity, bringing with it Morphea's dreams. Each night, she continues her work, weaving new visions for all who rest. Some dreams bring joy, others wisdom, and some—woven from darker threads—bring fear to teach courage.

This is why we dream: we wear Morphea's tapestries upon our sleeping minds.
